r1_mechanisms.md:32:### Problem and computational model
r1_mechanisms.md:36:The initial hard family is `N = p*q`, with independently generated, distinct primes of similar bit lengths. The factors are not given to the candidate algorithm. Also test prime powers, unbalanced factors, and composites with more than two prime factors.
r1_mechanisms.md:48:### 2.1 The polynomial and its first surviving coefficient
r1_mechanisms.md:107:### 2.2 A weaker extraction task already suffices: any surviving index
r1_mechanisms.md:133:### 2.3 Why individual coefficient queries do not provide the missing information
r1_mechanisms.md:143:### 2.4 Prefix testing and its factorial connection
r1_mechanisms.md:164:### 2.5 Ordinary differentiation loses the signal
r1_mechanisms.md:180:### 2.6 Scalar evaluations and sparse recovery have specific traps
r1_mechanisms.md:200:### Track A — Binary coefficient sections and compressed witness extraction
r1_mechanisms.md:242:### Track B — Quotient sketches that retain useful exponent information
r1_mechanisms.md:271:### Track C — Factorial unit tests and compressed recurrence evaluation
r1_mechanisms.md:285:### Track D — Hidden characteristic, lifted information, and annihilators
r1_mechanisms.md:307:### Track E — Transfer partial information into lattices or hidden relations
r1_mechanisms.md:333:### Track F — One independent connection
r1_mechanisms.md:339:## 4. Research procedure
r1_mechanisms.md:341:### Phase 1: establish the mathematical and literature baseline
r1_mechanisms.md:349:### Phase 2: write a candidate card before substantial coding
r1_mechanisms.md:351:Use this template for each serious candidate:
r1_mechanisms.md:372:### Phase 3: alternate construction and criticism
r1_mechanisms.md:382:Use a separate mathematical critic and complexity auditor if real subagents are available. Give the critic the candidate and code, not a persuasive narrative about expected success. Without subagents, perform separately documented review passes. Do not claim independent review unless it actually occurred.
r1_mechanisms.md:386:Do not stop after a survey or after one failed proposal. Complete at least one repair cycle on the best candidate, unless a complete proof and independently checked implementation are already obtained.
r1_mechanisms.md:388:### Phase 4: keep the result reproducible
r1_mechanisms.md:390:Preserve proofs, candidate versions, failed constructions, seeds, raw measurements, and exact commands. A negative result about one representation is useful when its scope is explicit and it points to a different operation to try.
r1_mechanisms.md:396:### Input families
r1_mechanisms.md:402:### Separate algorithm inputs from diagnostic truth
r1_mechanisms.md:404:The candidate receives only `N`, public configuration, and a random seed. A separate test harness may retain factors to compute labels and understand failures.
r1_mechanisms.md:406:Never leak factors through filenames, seeds deterministically encoding factors, candidate-specific parameter choices, precomputed group orders, smoothness-selected instances, library factorization calls, or a simulator initialized with the factors.
r1_mechanisms.md:408:Use actual random generation of the intended family and record the sampling procedure. Freeze candidate choices before the holdout evaluation. Special test families are useful but must not silently replace the intended distribution.
r1_mechanisms.md:410:### Record meaningful costs
r1_mechanisms.md:418:- Number of sketches, candidate combinations, restarts, and rejected trials.
r1_mechanisms.md:447:**Certificates.** A candidate factor is cheaply verifiable. That does not bound search time or certify an average-case/polynomial-time theorem. A decoder's correctness with ideal samples does not validate the source of those samples.
r1_mechanisms.md:465:    candidate_001.md
r1_mechanisms.md:466:    candidate_002.md
r1_mechanisms.md:473:    candidates/
r1_mechanisms.md:485:A candidate interface should accept `N` and public configuration only, return a factor or explicit failure, and emit an auditable metrics record. Reference factorization functions belong in the harness, never inside the purported new factorizer.
r1_mechanisms.md:529:Save this as `src/seed_checks.py`. It is reference/testing code using trial division and explicit coefficients. It is deliberately not a fast factoring algorithm and must not appear as the hidden implementation of a candidate.
r1_mechanisms.md:589:Read Sections 1–2, run the smoke tests, and create `STATUS.md`. Then investigate binary sectioning and weighted quotient sketches in parallel or as separate passes. Produce explicit candidate cards, implement the most discriminating small experiment for each, and perform a repair cycle after criticism.
r4_sos.md:7:3. **Mechanism hunt (Track F, independent).** Propose up to 3 concrete operations that would compute the second-informative-bit-type quantities from N using class-group or quadratic-form arithmetic. One possibility: the 4-rank / 8-rank of Cl(−4N) via Rédei matrices; Rédei matrices are known computable when the factorization is known. Identify exactly where the factorization is needed, and whether any part is computable from N alone (e.g. via reduced forms, the principal genus, ambiguous forms, which famously relate to factoring through Shanks' SQUFOF / Schnorr–Lenstra). Give candidate cards (brief §4 template) with cost and failure points. Be explicit about the known fact that ambiguous forms give factors, i.e. that finding an ambiguous form IS factoring.
PHASE1_NOTES.md:49:Counters instrument explicit quotient multiplication, GCD calls, and modular inversions; built-in modular exponentiation and GCD/inverse internal bit work are NOT expanded into ring operations. All raw records include N and diagnostic truth. No timing comparison or factoring speedup is claimed. The scalar/root/discriminant/CRT diagnostics deliberately enumerate small sets. The full seed harness explicitly materializes coefficients through degree1000; the candidate quotient degree is at most8, and the PIT demonstration uses degree at most12.

